-- title:
--   Puder 2014, Lemma 6.8 (π part) — π(w₁w₂) = π(w₁) + π(w₂) for disjoint words w₁, w₂ ∈ F_d
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 0$ and let $S_1, S_2$ be disjoint sets of generators of $\mathbf F_d$. Let $w_1 \in \mathbf F_d$ lie in the subgroup generated by $S_1$ and $w_2$ in the subgroup generated by $S_2$. Then
--   $$\pi(w_1 w_2) = \pi(w_1) + \pi(w_2),$$
--   where $\pi$ is the primitivity rank (`Puder.primitivityRank`, with values in $\{0, 1, 2, \dots\} \cup \{\infty\}$) and the sum is taken in $\mathbb N \cup \{\infty\}$, so it is $\infty$ when either summand is.
--
--   Puder 2014, p. 31: “Two words $w_1, w_2 \in \mathbf F_k$ are called *disjoint* (with respect to a given basis) if they share no common letters.” and “**Lemma 6.8.** *Let $w_1, w_2 \in \mathbf F_k$ be disjoint. Then $$\pi(w_1w_2) = \pi(w_1) + \pi(w_2)$$ $$\phi(w_1w_2) = \phi(w_1) + \phi(w_2)$$*”
--
--   *Formalization note.* “Disjoint” is taken here as: $w_1$ and $w_2$ lie in the subgroups generated by two disjoint sets of the basis elements $g_1, \dots, g_d$. The letters of the reduced word of $w_i$ form one such set. Only the first line of the lemma is stated. The second concerns Puder's $\phi$, a quantity defined through word measures on symmetric groups that the bundle does not define. Puder writes $\pi$ for subgroups. For $w \in \mathbf F_k$ it is the $\pi$ of $\langle w \rangle$, which is Puder 2015's Definition 2.2 that the bundle formalizes. The 2014 paper says it only sketches the proof (p. 32).
--
--   Source: D. Puder, *Primitive words, free factors and measure preservation*, Israel J. Math. 201 (2014) 25–73, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-013-0055-2 (arXiv:1104.3991, p. 31), Lemma 6.8, as cited in D. Puder, *Expansion of random graphs: new proofs, new results*, Invent. Math. 201 (2015) 845–908, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-014-0560-x (arXiv:1212.5216, pp. 37–38), proofs of Theorems 8.2 and 8.5.
-- source:
--   Puder, D., Primitive words, free factors and measure preservation, Israel J. Math. 201 (2014) 25–73, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-013-0055-2 (arXiv:1104.3991), p. 31, Lemma 6.8 (the identity for π), cited in Puder, D., Expansion of random graphs: new proofs, new results, Invent. Math. 201 (2015) 845–908, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-014-0560-x (arXiv:1212.5216), pp. 37–38

